  0. Definitions.

  "This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU General Public License.

  "Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds
of works, such as semiconductor masks.

  "The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License.  Each licensee is addressed as "you".  "Licensees" and
"recipients" refer to anyone who receives a copy according to this
License.

  "Modify" means to copy from or adapt all or part of the work in a
fashion requiring copyright permission, other than the making of an
exact copy.  The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the
earlier work or a work "based on" the earlier work.

  "Covered Work" means either the unmodified Program or a work based on
the Program.

  "Propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without
permission, would make you directly or indirectly liable for
infringement, except that Propagating a work does not include
distributing copies of the work to others.  "Convey" a work means any
kind of propagation that also enables other parties to make copies.
"Conveying" a work includes offering for download over a network.

  "Source Code" means the preferred form of the work for making
modifications to it.  "Object Code" means any non-source form of a
work.

  "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard or is widely used in the industry.

  "System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, other
than the work as a whole, that (a) is included in the normal form of
packaging a User Product, and (b) is intended only to enable the work
to function in its normal manner with the other parts of that User
Product.

  "Corresponding Source" for an Object Code work means all the source
code needed to generate, install, and run the object code and to modify
the work, including scripts to control those activities.  However, it
does not include the work's System Libraries, or general-purpose tools
or generally available free programs which are used unmodified in
performing those activities but which are not part of the work.

  1. Source Code.

  The Corresponding Source for a work in object code form is the source
code for the work plus any associated interface definition files,
scripts, and any other auxiliary files which are not specifically
excluded.  The Corresponding Source must be provided in a form
convenient for the user, with all information needed for compilation
and installation of the work.

